<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Yapese chiefdoms</h1><p class="st-meta">1500 CE – 1886 CE (approx.) · capital Gagil</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The ranked village chiefdoms of Yap in Micronesia, known for giant stone money and the sawei system that bound distant atolls to the district of Gagil.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Yap, a cluster of high islands in the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caroline-islands">Caroline Islands</a>, was settled over 2,000 years ago. By the time of European contact its villages had been arranged in a strict ranking, with three paramount villages heading rival alliances. Gachpar in the district of Gagil led the network of outer islands, a relationship known as the sawei.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Land, not persons, carried rank on Yap: each estate held a title, and chiefs of high villages commanded the services of low-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> villages. Through the sawei, atolls from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulithi">Ulithi</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namonuito">Namonuito</a> sent cloth, mats and shell valuables to Gagil, receiving food and protection against typhoons and sorcery in return. Yapese voyagers quarried huge limestone discs, rai, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palau">Palau</a> and carried them home as stone money, still used in ceremonial exchange. Taro swamps and fishing supported the population.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>A dispute between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> over Yap in 1885 was settled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-xiii">Pope Leo XIII</a> in Spain's favour, and Spanish rule began in 1886. Germany, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a> followed. Yap is now a state of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federated-states-of-micronesia">Federated States of Micronesia</a>, where chiefly councils keep a constitutional role.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="chiefdom, 1500 CE – 1886 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"/><path d="M68 20 V84"/><path d="M68 20 L63 30 H73 Z"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">chiefdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">North America and Oceania</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:89.55%;width:7.68%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1500 CE – 1886 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Gagil</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Yapese traditional religion, later Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Yapese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Micronesia</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Yap, Gagil, Sawei system</dd></dl></aside></div>

